
  Now back to the original motivation — "so we can track who logged in" — which I still can't pin down, and it's the
  last thing that could add a subsystem.

  [1mThe fork:[0m where login events are [3mrecorded[0m. Not where the ID is stored (settled), but who writes the "user X logged in
  at T" record. This matters now because we're already writing the backend contract, and adding a line to it is free
  today and a renegotiation later.

  - [1mServer records it; no client tracking code[0m — the session server already knows who authenticated and when; the
    contract simply says it logs that. Can't be blocked by an ad blocker, can't be forged, and it's the only version
    that's actually complete (it captures logins from any client, including ones you haven't built). Costs: you can't
    query it from the frontend, and it gives you nothing about in-page behavior.
  - [1mClient-side tracking module[0m — a new module with an event shape, emitting to an analytics endpoint. Buys you product
    analytics that the server can't see. Costs: it's a second new subsystem on top of the identity module, needs a
    destination you haven't named, and it under-reports by design.
  - [1mBoth[0m — server audit for truth, client events for product analytics. Complete, and the most work; the two record sets
    will disagree and someone will have to know why.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m "Track who logged in" is an audit question, and the authoritative answer lives where
  authentication happens. It's one clause in a contract we're writing anyway — zero client code. If you later want
  funnel analytics, that's a real feature with its own destination and its own design, and I'd rather not half-build it
  now on a guess about where the events go.
